description:
FILED s522a (Opus 5.5, 2026-10-19) while probing #3061.  SILENT WRONG, pre-existing (identical on the aff112d7 base extraction).

A named sub whose STRING says `"${e}[1]"` (or `"${e}{k}"`) -- in a string that is the SCALAR $e followed by the literal text "[1]" -- is not seen as capturing the file lexical $e, so the lexical is never promoted and the sub reads the empty package cell:

    my $e = 1;
    sub rf { return "${e}[1]" }
    print rf(), "\n";
    perl 5.40.3: 1[1]      PCL: [1]

`"${e}x"` is right (1x); `"${e}[1]"` at file level (no sub) is right; inside a sub with its own `my $e` it is right.  So the miss is the capture question only.

CAUSE (read, not yet fixed): `_canon_text_patterns` (Pl/Parser2.pm) attributes the TEXT `${e}[` to the ARRAY canon `@e` and `${e}{` to the HASH canon `%e` -- right in CODE (`${e}[1]` there IS `$e[1]`), wrong inside an interpolating string, where the braced name ends the variable.  A text regex cannot tell code from string; the standing rule is that interpolation scanning happens in Pl/InterpScan.pm, so the fix routes the string half of the capture question through InterpScan's variable events (the same route #2014 names for the braced refusal).  Related: #2014, #2645.

BAR: name resolution => the full sweep is the gate; gate-SET scan over both populations; a guard row inverse-verified.  Probe: scratch/s522a/pr/p9f.pl in the s522a worktree.
